      en: '• static: the default; the element is in normal flow and top/left have no effect.\n• relative: keeps its space in the flow but is visually offset from its original position. Its most common purpose is actually to become the reference for absolutely positioned descendants.\n• absolute: removed from the flow and positioned relative to the nearest non-static ancestor.\n• fixed: removed from the flow and positioned relative to the viewport, so it stays put while scrolling.\n• sticky: a hybrid — behaves as relative until it reaches a threshold, then sticks like fixed.\n\n```css\n.card { position: relative; }\n.badge { position: absolute; top: 8px; inset-inline-end: 8px; }\n\n.header { position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 10; }\n```\n\nCommon mistakes:\n1. An absolute element with no positioned ancestor jumps to the top of the page, because it falls back to the root.\n2. sticky does nothing without a top (or equivalent) offset, and breaks if any ancestor has overflow: hidden or auto — the single biggest source of confusion with it.\n3. fixed behaves differently if an ancestor has transform or filter, because those create a new containing block.',
