      en: 'CSS Grid is a two-dimensional layout system: it controls rows and columns together.\n\n```css\n.layout {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: 250px 1fr;\n  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto;\n  gap: 16px;\n  min-height: 100vh;\n}\n\n/* a responsive grid with no media queries */\n.cards {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(280px, 1fr));\n  gap: 24px;\n}\n```\n\nThat last rule deserves explanation: auto-fill with minmax creates as many columns as fit while keeping each at least 280px wide, distributing the remainder evenly — a fully responsive layout in one line with no breakpoints.\n\nThe fr unit means a fraction of the available space, which makes proportional distribution trivial.\n\nFor complex layouts there are named areas, which read extremely well:\n```css\n.page {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-areas:\n    "header header"\n    "sidebar main"\n    "footer footer";\n}\n.header { grid-area: header; }\n```\n\nGrid or Flexbox? Not a competition — typically Grid for the overall page and Flexbox inside components. The quick rule: if you are controlling rows and columns together it is Grid; if it is one direction it is Flexbox.\n\nThe conceptual difference: in Grid the parent dictates positions, while in Flexbox the children negotiate the space.',
